# Seeds who may log in, on a *new* deployment only.
#
# The allowlist lives in the database. This file is imported once, at
# startup, when the org has no administrator; after that it is ignored
# and the server says so. Manage the allowlist on the dashboard's
# Settings page, or through the API:
#
#   GET    /api/v1/allowlist
#   PUT    /api/v1/allowlist/{entry}   {"admin": true|false}
#   DELETE /api/v1/allowlist/{entry}
#
# where {entry} is an address or a GitHub account written @login.
#
# This file is the template and is what compose mounts by default. Its
# one entry is a placeholder nobody owns, so a default `docker compose
# up` grants access to no real person. To let yourself in:
#
#   cp deploy/allowlist.example deploy/allowlist
#   $EDITOR deploy/allowlist            # deploy/allowlist is gitignored
#   SPOV_ALLOWLIST_SOURCE=./allowlist docker compose -f deploy/compose.yaml up
#
# One entry per line: a GitHub account written @login, or an address,
# optionally followed by "admin". An account line is looked up on GitHub
# at the import and stored by the account's numeric ID, so a later
# rename does not change who it admits; a login GitHub does not know
# stops a first boot. An address matches only a GitHub account's
# primary, verified address. "#" starts a comment, and matching is
# case-insensitive.
#
#   @your-login admin
#   bob@example.com
#
# SIGHUP no longer reloads it — editing this file on a deployment that
# already has an administrator does nothing.
#
# @decision(DL-007) @decision(DL-128)
you@example.com admin
